# No stacked PRs

Every pull request targets `main`. This overrides any default, task
template, or cloud-agent setting that says to base new work on the current
feature branch. Use another base only when the user names it for that PR.

Why: a PR merged into a parent branch after that parent has already merged
never reaches `main`. In Sep 2026 this stranded 22 DePrize PRs (#1581 through
#1620, including free forecasts), plus #1508, #1540, #1554, #1329 and #1330.

## Do
- Branch from fresh `main`:
  `git fetch origin main && git checkout -b cursor/<name> origin/main`
- Open the PR with `base_branch: main`.
- Follow-up messages in the same task go on that same branch and the same PR.
  Commit and push there. Do not open another PR unless the user explicitly
  asks for a separate one.
- If the work needs another open PR, prefer one PR with both changes. If it
  must be separate, branch from `origin/main`, merge the other branch in, and
  still target `main`. Name the dependency in the PR body. The diff shrinks to
  your own commits once the dependency lands.
- Keep a long-running branch current by merging `origin/main` into it, not by
  basing new PRs on it.

## Don't
- Set a PR's base to a `cursor/*`, `feat/*`, `fix/*` or any other branch.
- Merge anything into a branch whose own PR has already merged or closed.
- Open a new PR for each user message, bug, or UI tweak. One task, one PR.
  A new sentence from the user is not a request for a new pull request.

## Existing stacks
Before merging a PR whose base is not `main`, check the base branch's PR. If
it has merged or closed, retarget this PR to `main` first.

```bash
gh pr list --state all --head <base-branch> --json number,state
```
